# herdr-client — an agent-aware terminal multiplexer installed on THIS
# machine. Client-side: no container, nothing deployed to a remote host.
#
# Everything here is optional; the defaults work.

# --- Version pinning ---

# ⚠️  THIS DOES NOT PIN THE DOWNLOAD, and an earlier version of this file
# said it did. Herdr's installer reads only HERDR_INSTALL_DIR and
# MANIFEST_URL; the version comes from https://herdr.dev/latest.json. A
# HERDR_VERSION piped into it is silently ignored.
#
# What it DOES do: on FAST, this is compared against the installed version
# and triggers a reinstall on a mismatch. So it holds a machine at whatever
# latest.json served when you last installed, rather than choosing what
# gets downloaded. Leave it at "latest" to reinstall only when missing.
#
# Why you might care: `herdr machine add` needs a compatible client/server
# pair, and an incompatible one triggers a setup that "asks before stopping
# it and its pane processes". The agent images install herdr the same way —
# latest at BUILD time — so if that prompt appears, CLEAN the agent
# environment to rebuild it against the same manifest.
#
# Format: a release version like 0.9.1, or "latest".
#HERDR_VERSION=latest

# --- Session generator ---
# (scripts/generate-session.sh — also on the deploy.sh action menu)

# Where the generated file is written. The generator reads each deployed
# agent environment's own .env for its SSH port and emits one pane per
# environment, labelled with the repo its workspace points at.
#
# Note it emits a SHELL SCRIPT, not a session.json: this repo has not
# verified Herdr's session schema or its pane-creation flags, and guessing a
# JSON schema produces something that looks right and silently fails. The
# generated script factors the herdr invocation into one function you confirm
# against `herdr --help` once. See scripts/generate-session.sh.
# Format: an absolute path.
#HERDR_SESSION_OUTPUT=$HOME/.config/herdr/open-fleet.sh

# Host the generated panes should SSH to. Leave unset for localhost, which
# is correct when Herdr runs on the same machine that hosts the containers.
#
# Note the generator emits SSH panes rather than `docker exec` panes even
# for local containers. That is deliberate: one pane shape then covers both
# local and remote, and it keeps the account running Herdr — and therefore
# Collie, and therefore your phone — pointed at an unprivileged container
# account rather than at the container runtime. See README's "Why SSH panes".
# Format: a hostname or IP.
#HERDR_TARGET_HOST=localhost
